...ne; most of those who were brought died, and also of hunger. And in this present pestilence the same thing happens: and in all [plagues] that occur, it will be the same, until they are finished off. And if there had been attention and care taken, so that these Indians had been instructed in grammar, logic, natural philosophy, and medicine, they could have given aid to many of those who have died: because in this city of Mexico, we see with our own eyes, that those who can be bled and purged, as is proper, and in time, recover; while the rest die: and since the Spanish physicians and bloodletters, who could cure them, are few, they can only help a few: and now they themselves are almost worn out, sick, and dying—the bloodletters and physicians: there is no longer anyone who can, or who takes care to go and help the poor Indians: and so they die for lack of remedy or help.

[List of Nahuatl terms for body parts, each followed by descriptive qualifying words — e.g., breast (chichioalli): sour, hard, heart-like; pregnant woman's breast: with a pointed nipple, firm, exuding milk, hanging, (further descriptive terms for breasts of different kinds, firm, soft, drooping, pendulous, etc.);
thigh (metztli): with a broad thigh, flowered, wrinkled, fleshy, trembling, large;
hip/haunch (itzotzolli): with fleshy, firm, fat flesh, flowered, round, flowering, rounded, soft;
leg (acaluihiantli): canoe-shaped, canoe-like, thunderous, fleshy like a rabbit's, canoe-like;
knee (icomoliuhiantli): knobby, box-like, knobbed, boxed;
rib bone (chiquiuhiotl): with ribs, bony, entirely of bone;
chest (ichiquiniti): with a chest that is strong, hardened, green, verdant, tender;
rib cage (omicicuilli): thick, thin, soft, very soft, curved, bent, curved further, bent further;
small rib/sternum (itepicicitli): small-ribbed, resounding, rattling, thunderous.]
